+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+`src/pointer.ts` has `resolvePointer` and a private `parse` that validates RFC 6901 syntax and unescapes segments. It resolves only existing values, so it cannot locate a parent plus final key for `add`. The library has no runtime dependencies. See proposal.md for motivation.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+- Exact RFC 6902 semantics, including its error cases, with no mutation of inputs.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+- Performance tuning for large documents; no in-place variant.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- **Reuse pointer parsing.** Export `parse` from `pointer.ts` (internal; not re-exported from `index.ts`) and have `patch.ts` convert its `JsonPointerError` into `JsonPatchError`. Alternative: duplicate the parser — rejected to keep one definition of pointer syntax.
+- **Clone once, then mutate the clone.** The document is deep-cloned up front and operations mutate the clone; values taken from the patch (`add`/`replace`) and `copy` sources are deep-cloned on insertion. Alternative: path copying per operation — more efficient but more complex; rejected for simplicity and obvious isolation.
+- **Own-members and `__proto__` safety.** Objects are cloned by creating plain objects and assigning with `Object.defineProperty`, and lookups use `Object.hasOwn`, so `__proto__` is an ordinary key.
+- **Validate the whole patch first**, then apply, so malformed patches fail before any work and error messages can name the operation index.
+- **Move** = read value at `from`, reject if `from` is a proper prefix of `path` (compare segment arrays), no-op if equal, else remove then add. **Copy** = deep clone of source, then add.
+- **Test equality** uses a custom recursive JSON equality (order-insensitive objects, ordered arrays, `===` for primitives; `-0` equals `0`).
+- **Errors**: a new `JsonPatchError` class, exported from `index.ts`, mirroring `JsonPointerError`.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [Deep clone cost on big documents] → acceptable; correctness and isolation first.
+- [Non-JSON inputs (undefined, functions, cycles)] → undefined behaviour; inputs are assumed to be JSON values.
+- [Remove-then-add of `move` when path indices refer to the post-removal array] → matches RFC 6902 §4.4; covered by a test.
